**Reused Evidence**
None. No prior accepted item genuinely carries over to Chapter 5B.

**New Evidence**
Overall, the 2022 record is mixed on short-run growth but strongly negative on macro stability, policy credibility, and household purchasing power. The most useful evidence is below.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Global and Domestic Challenges Weigh on Outlook for Turkish Economy in 2022","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-02-25","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/02/25/global-and-domestic-challenges-weigh-on-outlook-for-turkish-economy-in-2022","claim":"Frequent changes in monetary policy, epitomized by rate cuts since September 2021, sent the lira to historic lows and inflation to record highs, and these challenges eroded real incomes for poorer households.","locator":"press release body","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct World Bank country press release with a concrete, dated claim about policy, currency, inflation, and household welfare.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Targets the 2022 outlook and directly describes the policy environment entering 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Strongly attributable to Erdogan because the rate-cut policy and monetary stance were his signature economic choices.","contrary_evidence":["The same release notes exports had grown strongly in 2021 and remained part of the growth story."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Turkey Economic Monitor: Sailing Against the Tide","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-02-25","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/country/turkey/publication/economic-monitor","claim":"The World Bank said Turkey was experiencing rising macro-financial volatility with impacts on households and that tighter monetary policy was warranted to restore investor confidence and anchor inflation expectations.","locator":"story highlights and taking stock section","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct World Bank country monitor with a clear policy assessment and household-impact framing.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Early-2022 snapshot of the ruler-period and its inherited macro position.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Erdogan government as the policy environment under review, though this is partly a baseline assessment rather than a discrete 2022 decision record.","contrary_evidence":["The monitor also noted a strong 2021 rebound in output and exports."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Türkiye: OECD Economic Outlook, Volume 2022 Issue 1","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2022-06-08","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/oecd-economic-outlook/volume-2022/issue-1_62d0ca31-en/full-report/component-49.html","claim":"Employment had recovered to pre-pandemic levels and income had been boosted by a 50% minimum wage increase, but prices were rising further because of accommodative monetary policy, commodity prices, and exchange-rate depreciation, eroding real incomes.","locator":"Türkiye section, lines around 4009","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"OECD surveillance text with specific labor, wage, inflation, and policy statements for the target year.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022 economic conditions and policy mix.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Erdogan because the accommodative monetary stance was the government-backed framework in place during 2022.","contrary_evidence":["The minimum wage increase provided some near-term household support."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Türkiye: OECD Economic Outlook, Volume 2022 Issue 2","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2022-11-22","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/oecd-economic-outlook/volume-2022/issue-2_f6da2159-en/full-report/component-51.html","claim":"Real GDP grew by 7.5% in the first half of 2022, tourism fully recovered, and exporters benefited from supply-chain disruption, but investment remained subdued and macroeconomic imbalances rose.","locator":"Türkiye section, lines around 4019-4020","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"OECD country surveillance with specific 2022 growth, tourism, investment, and imbalance data.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-year evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to Erdogan because the policy environment shaped investment and stabilization conditions, though some of the growth was externally driven.","contrary_evidence":["The same passage says investment activity was subdued and imbalances rose."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Türkiye: OECD Economic Outlook, Volume 2022 Issue 2","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2022-11-22","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/oecd-economic-outlook/volume-2022/issue-2_f6da2159-en/full-report/component-51.html","claim":"Consumer price inflation reached 85.5% and producer price inflation 158% by October 2022, while the minimum wage was raised by 30% in July after a 50% increase and lira depreciation raised import-price pressures.","locator":"Türkiye section, lines around 4021","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"OECD gives concrete inflation and wage figures for the target year, tied to policy and exchange-rate conditions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 economic outcomes.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Erdogan-era macro policy because the inflation and depreciation sit within the low-rate regime he defended.","contrary_evidence":["The minimum-wage increase modestly cushioned purchasing power."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.8","5B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Türkiye: OECD Economic Outlook, Volume 2022 Issue 2","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2022-11-22","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/oecd-economic-outlook/volume-2022/issue-2_f6da2159-en/full-report/component-51.html","claim":"The government introduced measures to shield vulnerable groups from rising energy and food prices, including doubled energy assistance for 4 million households and lower electricity tariffs for low-consumption users.","locator":"Türkiye section, lines around 4046-4047","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"OECD country surveillance with specific distributional relief measures and household counts.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers the 2022 policy response to the cost-of-living shock.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Erdogan government because it was a state response during his tenure, though it is mitigation rather than structural reform.","contrary_evidence":["The same report warns that non-targeted price supports and energy dependence kept fiscal and inflation risks high."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Staff Completes 2022 Article IV Mission to Turkiye","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-11-04","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/11/04/pr22372-turkiye-imf-staff-completes-2022-article-iv-mission-to-turkiye","claim":"The IMF mission said policy-rate cuts in late 2021 added to existing vulnerabilities, were followed by lira depreciation and high inflation, and that it recommended early policy-rate hikes and stronger central-bank independence.","locator":"mission statement paragraphs 3-4","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous IMF mission statement, directly targeted to the 2022 consultation and policy mix.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct target-period consultation statement from late 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Strongly attributable to Erdogan because the rate-cut path and institutional stance were government-driven policy choices.","contrary_evidence":["The statement also notes strong post-pandemic growth and a dynamic private sector."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Republic of Turkiye: 2022 Article IV Consultation-Press Release; and Staff Report","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2023-08-17","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/publications/cr/issues/2023/08/17/republic-of-trkiye-2022-article-iv-consultation-press-release-and-staff-report-538023","claim":"The IMF summarized the new policy model as relying on low interest rates, directing lending to selected sectors, and increasingly distortionary macrofinancial and regulatory measures after the lira ran under severe pressure; it also said inflation reached 85% in October 2022 and fiscal risks were rising.","locator":"Summary, lines 267-270","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official IMF country report with a concise synthesis of the 2022 policy model, its consequences, and associated risks.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective but still about the 2022 consultation and 2022 outcomes.","ruler_attribution":"Strongly attributable to Erdogan because the low-rate model, directed credit, and intervention-heavy response were core government choices.","contrary_evidence":["The same summary notes the public-debt burden remained low compared with peers."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.3","5B.4","5B.5","5B.7","5B.9","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Turkey's new finance minister in the eye of a storm","publisher":"Reuters via eKathimerini","publication_date":"2022-01-17","url":"https://www.ekathimerini.com/economy/1175654/turkeys-new-finance-minister-in-the-eye-of-a-storm/","claim":"Reuters reported that Nureddin Nebati, chosen by Erdogan to oversee and articulate the low-rate policy, had little direct economic training and replaced the last prominent orthodox-policy figure in the cabinet.","locator":"paragraphs around lines 120-122","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting is contemporaneous and specific, though the mirror is secondary hosting rather than the wire itself.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022 and relevant to the ruler-period economic team.","ruler_attribution":"Strong attribution to Erdogan because the appointment was his and it signaled the type of economic team he preferred.","contrary_evidence":["Supporters said Nebati was working closely with the president and had helped stem the lira collapse."],"lenses":["5B.2","5B.7"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Erdogan says interest rates will be lowered and inflation will fall too","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-01-29","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/erdogan-says-interest-rates-will-be-lowered-and-inflation-will-fall-too-2752504","claim":"Erdogan repeated that interest rates would be lowered further and that inflation would fall as a result, describing the economic shock as temporary even as Turkey was in a currency crisis.","locator":"Reuters wire text in article body","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters wire reporting, but accessed through a syndication mirror.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 statement by the ruler.","ruler_attribution":"Direct ruler attribution because the quote is Erdogan's own public economic guidance.","contrary_evidence":["The article notes the central bank had already slashed rates 500 basis points since September and inflation was nearing a 20-year high."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.3","5B.7"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Analysis-Fallout of Ukraine war exposes Turkey's vulnerable economy","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-03-10","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/analysisfallout-of-ukraine-war-exposes-turkeys-vulnerable-economy-2782597","claim":"Reuters reported that Erdogan's bet that Turkey could ride out the inflation and currency shock with low rates and reserves had collapsed after the Ukraine war left the economy vulnerable to soaring energy prices, and that he was betting lower rates would reverse chronic trade deficits through exports and tourism.","locator":"Reuters wire text around lines 429-445","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters analysis with clear causal claims and explicit attribution to Erdogan's policy bet.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 crisis-year evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Strongly attributable to Erdogan because the policy bet and rate-cut logic are explicitly described as his.","contrary_evidence":["The article also notes costly market interventions and deposit protections briefly stabilized the lira in January."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"CBRT - 2022 Annual Report","publisher":"Central Bank of the Republic of Turkiye","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www3.tcmb.gov.tr/yillikrapor/2022/en/m-1-1.html","claim":"The report states that the president has authority to determine the salaries and allowances of the governor, deputy governors, board members, MPC members, and auditing-committee members, and that the 2022 board and MPC remained in place under Governor Sahap Kavcioglu.","locator":"section 1.1.1 Board and 1.1.5 Financial Benefits Provided to Senior Management","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official institutional record for the central bank's 2022 governance structure and statutory hierarchy.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly describes the 2022 governance arrangement.","ruler_attribution":"This is structural attribution rather than a discrete policy act; it shows formal presidential leverage over central-bank leadership conditions.","contrary_evidence":["The report also shows the CBRT had a formal board, MPC meetings, and statutory procedures."],"lenses":["5B.2","5B.3","5B.5"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
- I reviewed roughly 25 to 30 documents across World Bank, OECD, IMF, CBRT, Reuters wire mirrors, and a few Turkish and regional mirrors.
- I retained the sources that best distinguish target-year policy, implementation, and consequences: low-rate monetary policy, inflation and lira collapse, household impacts, wage relief, and appointment style.
- I rejected a number of duplicates and near-duplicates, especially Reuters syndications repeating the same Nebati or rate-cut story, because they added no new material claim.
- I rejected out-of-period materials from 2021 when they did not materially carry into 2022, unless they were needed as baseline context, and I did not bring in 2023-2024 turnaround pieces because they would blur the 2022 target period.
- I rejected general macro datasets and indicator pages already covered by the supplied local prior, so I did not re-fetch WDI, WGI, CPI, BTI, or similar structured series.
- I also rejected Turkey-Syria and other foreign-policy items from earlier chapters because they do not answer Chapter 5B unless they directly change domestic economic welfare.

**Remaining Gaps**
- The biggest gap is direct evidence on the ruler's exact 2022 appointment choices inside the economic team beyond the finance-minister case and broad central-bank governance structure.
- More target-year evidence on household median income, inflation by region, and inequality would sharpen the distribution lens.
- More direct evidence on corruption, favoritism, and politically connected credit allocation would help the 5B.5 and 5B.4 lenses, but the current dossier already shows a strong macro-instability and credibility problem.
- Additional search would likely add nuance, not reverse the basic picture: short-run growth and some relief measures coexisted with a deeply unstable, low-credibility macro policy regime.
